OpenWRT incremented snapshot backup}
\par \pard\plain \s19\loch\sl276\slmult1\sb0\sa140\ql\ltrpar{\loch
Busyback creates a snapshot view of the filesystems to be backed up by combining a previous backup with recent changes. }{\loch
It }{\loch
runs in }{\loch
OpenWRT }{\loch
busybox}{\loch
 and it}{\loch
s}{\loch
 processing relies }{\loch
o}{\loch
n sta}{\loch
n}{\loch
dard tool that come with th}{\loch
e}{\loch
 OS }{\loch
of the OpenWRT router (plus little standard extentitions). }{\loch
It is a lean and small tool, this documentation i}{\loch
s}{\loch
 the largest file }{\loch
in the repository}{\loch
, }{\loch
alltgether ist about 150 kB. }{\loch
Busyback }{\loch
is not neede}{\loch
d}{\loch
 to access backuped data.}
\par \pard\plain \s19\loch\sl276\slmult1\sb0\sa140\ql\ltrpar{\loch
For convenience the system storing the backup (e.g. the OpenWRT router or other systems that run busybox) is called  the \u8222\'84backup server\u8220\'93}{\loch
.  }{\loch
In contrast, the server/system, that is subject to the backup, is named the \u8222\'84backup client\u8220\'93}
\par \pard\plain \s19\loch\sl276\slmult1\sb0\sa140\ql\ltrpar{\loch\b\loch
Separation of Concerns (Encryption):}{\loch
 }{\loch
busyback does not include }{\loch
encryption. }{\loch
Encryption is to be}{\loch
 handled at the OS/storage layer (e.g., via LUKS dm-crypt containers). The backup script remains agnostic to the underlying crypto layout, operating solely on the }{\loch
unlocked}{\loch
, mounted filesystem block device.}
\par \pard\plain \s19\loch\sl276\slmult1\sb0\sa140\ql\ltrpar{\loch\b\loch
Core Engine (Rsync):}{\loch
 }{\loch
Busyback }{\loch
orchestrates the target layout by utilizing native }{\loch\cs15\rtlch\af6 \ltrch\hich\af6\loch\f6\dbch\af8\loch
rsync}{\loch
 for differentials. It relies heavily on standard filesystems supporting hard links to create space-efficient, point-in-time snapshot trees.}
\par \pard\plain \s19\loch\sl276\slmult1\sb0\sa140\ql\ltrpar{\loch
Busyback has a dry run feature as default. To execute backups add \u8222\'84go\u8220\'93 as last command line paramter, else it is a dry }{\loch
r}{\loch
un and does not change anything on disk.  }
